[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":112},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1793672466000,"id","cy90zbdehwxv5memq7aqw725b","title","Cisco Talos 分析 AI 驱动型 Windows 信息窃取器 VOLTA","originalTitle","VOLTA, the infostealer that asks an AI which victims are worth the effort","summary","Cisco Talos 分析了 Windows 信息窃取器 VOLTA-AUTODOXER。该程序窃取浏览器密码、Cookie、支付卡、加密钱包种子短语、OAuth tokens、SSH 与云凭据等数据，并借助 LLM 生成受害者资产估值、安全习惯评估及可变现信息分级；默认调用 Claude Opus 4.6，失败后切换 Kimi K2 和 Gemini 2.5 Flash。","reason","source",{"_77":111},"links",{"_61":110},"publishedAt","2026-11-02T18:34:13.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-02T19:56:36.793Z","timelineAt","category","activity","tags",[82,80,78,105,106,107,108,109],"score",67,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_92":93,"_13":94},"x","readingMode","full","author","Pierluigi Paganini","body",{"_101":-5,"_61":102,"_103":-5,"_104":36},"outline",[95],"relatedStories",[91],"topics",[68,69,70,71,72,73,74],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","fAFp_Yp6uqsI","reportCount",4,"additionalSourceCount",3,{"_75":89,"_77":90},{"_75":87,"_77":88},{"_75":85,"_77":86},{"_75":83,"_77":84},{"_75":81,"_77":82},{"_75":79,"_77":80},{"_75":76,"_77":78},"slug","ai-security","name","AI 安全","malware","恶意软件","attack","攻击活动","ai-agent","AI Agent 安全","black-market","数据窃取与黑产","ransomware","勒索软件","microsoft","Microsoft / 微软",{"_92":93,"_13":94},"publicId","41881cfd-565d-48f3-8331-58892c484e4a","Cisco披露VOLTA用AI评估窃密受害者",{"_11":96,"_97":98,"_99":100},"sec-1","text","VOLTA is an AI-powered infostealer that steals personal data, then uses LLMs to ","level",2,"zh","<h2 id=\"sec-1\">VOLTA is an AI-powered infostealer that steals personal data, then uses LLMs to assess victims’ value and rank targets for attackers.</h2><p>Cisco Talos has analyzed a new Windows infostealer called VOLTA, which calls itself VOLTA-AUTODOXER. It steals the usual data, then hands the loot to a large language model that ranks what the victim is worth. Ryan Fetterman laid it out in a <a href=\"https://blog.talosintelligence.com/appraisal-by-ai-model-driven-victim-prioritization-in-the-volta-infostealer/\">Talos report</a>, after the malware turned up through the CAIRN research effort.</p><p><em>“There is a new idea in the cybercrime world — cast a wide net, steal everything, and let AI sort out the specifics. A new malware self-named “VOLTA-AUTODOXER,” surfaced through the <a href=\"https://blog.talosintelligence.com/introducing-cairn-frontier-tracking-for-ai-integrated-malware/\"><u>CAIRN research effort</u></a>, is a self-deleting Windows infostealer that augments its post-theft workflow with an LLM-powered victim-profiling stage.” reads the <a href=\"https://blog.talosintelligence.com/appraisal-by-ai-model-driven-victim-prioritization-in-the-volta-infostealer/\">report</a> published by Talos.</em></p><figure><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/securityaffairs.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-1.png?ssl=1\"><img width=\"1000\" height=\"665\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/9f97663069e8af17.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/528b7f8bc4df5b7c.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/3162a1cd6721a430.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/9f97663069e8af17.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\"></a></figure><p>The malware deletes itself after running and does not use servers controlled by the attacker. Instead, it uses third-party systems to carry out its activities.</p><p>The malicious code supports data-stealing features are advanced. It collects browser passwords, cookies and saved payment card details from Chromium browsers, as well as data from Firefox, Thunderbird and Outlook. It also steals Slack workspace information and login sessions from Discord, Telegram, Steam, Battle.net and Roblox.</p><p>The malware also collects Microsoft OAuth tokens, SSH keys, cloud service credentials, screenshots and all saved Wi-Fi passwords. For crypto wallets, it tries to crack the wallet password directly on the infected device and saves the results in its report. To steal some browser tokens, it must bypass Chrome’s App-Bound Encryption.</p><p>But VOLTA goes further. It also builds a detailed profile of the victim. This includes their location and home address, employer and company domain, estimated year of birth, the last four digits of payment cards, Slack workspaces and information about who created documents. It also looks for signs of iPhone backups and checks the current value of the victim’s Roblox account.</p><p><em>“After harvesting the standard stealer haul, including browser passwords; cookies; payment cards; cryptocurrency wallet seed phrases; Discord, Telegram, Steam, and Roblox sessions; Microsoft OAuth tokens; SSH and cloud-provider keys; Wi-Fi profiles; and screenshots, VOLTA builds a structured evidence bundle about the person behind the machine.” states the report.</em></p><p>It uses the victim’s own logged-in sessions to fill the gaps. It searches Gmail for receipts from Amazon, DoorDash, Uber Eats, Grubhub, Walmart and PayPal to confirm a home address, pulls order histories from Uber Eats and DoorDash, and adds detail to the victim’s Discord profile. Even a takeout history can now count as evidence.</p><p>The bundle goes to OpenRouter, a routing service for AI models, with a system prompt that tells the model to act as a forensic intelligence analyst drafting a target assessment. </p><p><em>“The required output includes a section titled “WHAT THEY’RE WORTH” with valuables ranked by monetization with a low/medium/high tier, and a section on “THEIR OPSEC” containing an assessment of the victim’s security posture and password hygiene.” states the report. “The model is the operator’s triage analyst; it turns a raw credential log into a prioritized and citation-backed dossier of the victim and their monetizable assets.”</em></p><p>It also needs a section called “THEIR OPSEC”, rating the victim’s security habits and password hygiene. In short, it’s a performance review for the person it just robbed.</p><p>The prompt asks for something agents do well: connecting scattered facts. It pushes the model to link names, addresses or universities to patterns in the victim’s passwords. The model isn’t hunting for new data, only stretching what the malware already took. The operator wants a short, blunt assessment with a trail of citations.</p><p>By default VOLTA calls Anthropic’s <a href=\"https://securityaffairs.com/189131/ai/anthropic-claude-opus-ai-model-discovers-22-firefox-bugs.html\">Claude Opus 4.6</a>. If the model refuses, which VOLTA spots by scanning replies for phrases like “I can’t” or “I’m not able”, it moves on to Moonshot AI’s Kimi K2 and then Google’s Gemini 2.5 Flash. A low temperature setting of 0.2 keeps the output consistent. </p><figure><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/securityaffairs.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/image-2.png?ssl=1\"><img width=\"1000\" height=\"147\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/f5f681085fb10b2e.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/1600b5d22aa9383f.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/0fd6a113c7638a9f.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/f5f681085fb10b2e.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\"></a></figure><p>The requests are hand-built JSON with no software kit, and the malware still runs without the AI stage if no API key is present.</p><p>Talos sees the fallback system as evidence that the malware’s author expected security protections and designed ways to bypass them.</p><p>The AI analysis runs directly on the victim’s computer, using their own internet connection, before any data is sent out. The malware creates a file called Identity_Report.txt, receives the AI’s response in AI_Analysis.txt, and sends both files together in a ZIP archive called volta_dox.zip.</p><p>The stolen data can be sent to the attacker in up to three stages. The first archive, volta_log.zip, contains the information collected by the malware. The second, volta_dox.zip, contains the AI analysis. A third archive, volta_doxnet.zip, is created if the malware finds additional network information. The attacker receives everything through a Telegram channel.</p><p>The malware also has six configurable fields that can be changed when it is built. This suggests that its author may offer a builder service, allowing customers to create their own customised versions.</p><p>The credentials stored in these fields are encrypted, but the encryption is weak because the key is stored inside the malware itself. Anyone who analyses the file can recover them.</p><p>If VOLTA is sold as a commercial product, its AI feature could allow even inexperienced criminals to identify valuable victims and receive detailed, ranked profiles directly through Telegram.</p><p>For defense, Talos suggests an application control policy that restricts which processes can launch Chrome with the –headless flag, which would block the App-Bound Encryption bypass. At minimum, alert when Chrome starts with –headless from any process outside a known developer set. Storing credentials in hardware-backed stores also helps.</p><p>If you’re compromised, the main program may have deleted itself, but try to keep %APPDATA%\\volta_debug.txt. It’s a timestamped log of what was collected and whether the AI call worked. Revoke any exposed tokens and change passwords. If a wallet’s seed phrase sat in plaintext on disk, treat the wallet as fully compromised and move the funds to a new wallet whose seed was generated on an air-gapped device.</p><p><em>“VOLTA is a mature stealer with a late-stage AI layer added for one purpose: relieving the operator of analytical work. The axis of AI improvement here is not speed or scale but effort displacement; the same logic visible in <a href=\"https://securityaffairs.com/tag/closedquorum\">CLOSEDQUORUM</a>, though applied to a different phase.” concludes the post. “These implants reflect the same underlying logic: Identify the step that requires the most sustained human attention and replace it with a model call. The question for defenders is not whether this pattern will appear elsewhere, but which phase it touches next.”</em></p><p><strong>Follow me on Twitter:&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://twitter.com/securityaffairs\"><strong>@securityaffairs</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/sec.affairs\"><strong>Facebook</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;</strong><a href=\"https://infosec.exchange/@securityaffairs\"><strong>Mastodon</strong></a><strong></strong></p><p><a href=\"http://www.linkedin.com/pub/pierluigi-paganini/b/742/559\"><strong>Pierluigi&nbsp;Paganini</strong></a><strong></strong></p><p><strong>(</strong><a href=\"http://securityaffairs.co/wordpress/\"><strong>SecurityAffairs</strong></a><strong>&nbsp;–&nbsp;hacking,&nbsp;malware)</strong></p>","zhKind","complete","数据窃取","大模型","Microsoft","Google","Mozilla","https://securityaffairs.com/202281/malware/volta-the-infostealer-that-asks-an-ai-which-victims-are-worth-the-effort.html","Security Affairs",{"_113":114,"_115":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
